 03_1	We built E from its power series. Why should adding inputs multiply outputs? Put the terms of E of x across the top, and the terms of E of y down the side. Each square contains one product. The complete grid goes on forever. We are showing its first corner, not pretending that sixteen products are the whole answer.
 03_2	Instead of collecting the products by rows, collect them by total degree. Degree zero has just one square, containing one. Degree one has two squares: x and y. Their sum is x plus y. Now inspect the three squares of degree two. Before we combine them, can you recognize their sum?
 03_3	They give x squared over two, plus x y, plus y squared over two. Factor out one half. Inside the brackets is x squared plus two x y plus y squared: the square of x plus y. So this diagonal contributes x plus y, squared, divided by two. The factorial in the denominator has survived.
-03_4	The degree-three diagonal works the same way. In general, multiplying a degree-m diagonal by m factorial gives the binomial coefficients. The binomial theorem combines that diagonal into x plus y to the m, divided by m factorial. We have shown the complete diagonals through degree three here. Higher ones continue beyond the visible corner.
-03_5	There is one analytic permission slip. Both series converge absolutely, so their product can be regrouped by diagonals without changing its sum. The diagonals are precisely the terms of E at x plus y. Therefore E of x times E of y equals E of x plus y. The multiplication law came from the factorials and convergence, not from naming the function exponential.
+03_4	The degree-three diagonal works the same way. In general, multiplying a degree-m diagonal by m factorial gives the binomial coefficients. The binomial theorem combines that diagonal into the sum x plus y, raised to the m, divided by m factorial. We have shown the complete diagonals through degree three here. Higher ones continue beyond the visible corner.
+03_5	There is one analytic permission slip. Both series converge absolutely, so their product can be regrouped by diagonals without changing its sum. The diagonals are precisely the terms of E at the sum, x plus y. Therefore E of x times E of y equals E of the sum, x plus y. The multiplication law came from the factorials and convergence, not from naming the function exponential.
 03_6	Set y equal to minus x. The product is E of zero, which is one, so E never vanishes. For a real x, apply the same law to two copies of half x. E of x is the square of a nonzero real number, so it is positive. Since its derivative is itself, it is increasing. Those familiar properties now follow from what we built.
+03_7	Now we can name it. Call E of one by the letter e. The law says E of two is e times e, and E of three is e cubed. Roots give fractions, reciprocals give negatives, and continuity fills in the rest. That is when writing E of x as e to the x becomes a result, not a label.
